    <example number="7.64">
      <problem>
        <p>Factor completely: $$6y^{2} - 18y - 60$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step guide demonstrating how to factor a trinomial polynomial, from identifying the GCF to checking the final factored form.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>Is there a GCF?</cell>
              <cell>Yes, 6.</cell>
              <cell>$$6y^{2} - 18y - 60$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Factor out the GCF.</cell>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$6\left( {y^{2} - 3y - 10} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>In the parentheses, is it a binomial, trinomial,or are there more terms?</cell>
              <cell>Trinomial with leading coefficient 1.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>“Undo” FOIL.</cell>
              <cell>$$6\left( {y\quad} \right)\left( {y\quad} \right)$$</cell>
              <cell>$$6\left( {y + 2} \right)\left( {y - 5} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Check your answer.</cell>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Is the expression factored completely?</cell>
              <cell />
              <cell>Yes.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Neither binomial is a difference of squares.</cell>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Multiply.$$\begin{array}{l} \\ \\ {\mspace{45mu} 6\left( {y + 2} \right)\left( {y - 5} \right)} \\ {\mspace{45mu} 6\left( {y^{2} - 5y + 2y - 10} \right)} \\ {\mspace{45mu} 6\left( {y^{2} - 3y - 10} \right)} \\ {\mspace{45mu} 6y^{2} - 18y - 60\ ✓} \end{array}$$</cell>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
